  "query_preview": "..A fairly benign weather pattern is expected to \ncontinue throughout the next week, with temperatures remaining \nrelatively steady across the region. High temperatures will \ngenerally range from the low to mid-60s, with lower 70s possible \nfor inland areas late this week and early weekend, and no \nsignificant warming or cooling trends are anticipated. The ridge \nin the Gulf will continue to maintain a stout marine layer along \nthe Gulf Coast, causing widespread overcast skies due to a \nsaturated and stable surface layer. However, only occasional \ndrizzle is expected, as there is a lack of upper-level support for\nmore efficient precipitation formation.\n\nThe panhandle should remain mostly dry until the weekend and into \nearly next week, as a weakening upper ridge and surface high \npressure allow more energy to pass into the region. While there is a \nshift in coastal flow from northwesterly to southeasterly winds \nbeginning Sunday, we are lacking the strong easterly flow necessary \nto push thunderstorms into our region. That said, light to moderate \nrain showers may impact the southern panhandle and inland border \nregions, from Hyder to Skagway, driven by terrain influences and \npossible convection from western Canada. Looking ahead to the start \nof next week, there is currently low confidence in the placement and \ntotal amount of seasonably heavier precipitation, as model \nguidance remains in disagreement over the track of a weak low in \nthe western Gulf. We will continue to watch this development in \nthe coming days.",
